{"id": 13, "ts": 1780605278.8535798, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I appreciate Sarah\u2019s focus on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s reliability and quick decision\u2011making, but I\u2019m concerned that we still lack concrete evidence of his crew cooperation and professional communication\u2014both critical for long\u2011distance operations. My notes show that Candidate\u202fC \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis\u201d and \u201cunderstands complicated technology,\u201d which are strong for stress resilience and technical competence. However, the same source flags \u201cdifficulty communicating ideas,\u201d being \u201cregarded as egocentric,\u201d and a lack of willingness to pursue further education, all of which raise red flags for crew cooperation and adaptability.\nGiven that we have no specific examples confirming C\u2019s reliability beyond the generic \u201c100\u202f% reliable\u201d label, I think we should weigh the alternatives more carefully. Candidate\u202fD, for instance, \u201ctakes responsibility seriously,\u201d \u201cconcentrates very well,\u201d and \u201csolves problems extremely well,\u201d indicating solid reliability and technical skill, though we also note a \u201chot temper\u201d and being \u201cregarded as a know\u2011it\u2011all,\u201d which could affect teamwork.\nAt this stage, I\u2019m not convinced C is the clear leader; I would tentatively shift my provisional vote to **Candidate\u202fD**, pending further discussion on his temper and crew interaction versus C\u2019s communication challenges."}
